{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RMb6opPKGxU8D2f7fBzsS5","type":"tool_result","content":"154\t## Step 5: Visual Illustrations\n155\t\n156\tLessons should be visually rich, not walls of text. A learner who scrolls past three screen-heights of unbroken prose has already disengaged. Use **inline markdown images** inside `theory` sections for supporting visuals, and the dedicated **`image` widget** (US-050) as a standalone section for hero illustrations, diagrams, or figures that warrant their own caption + attribution treatment.\n157\t\n158\tPlan visuals during this step — *before* you write the section JSON — so you can weave the image references naturally into the markdown rather than bolting them on at the end.\n159\t\n160\t### Inline markdown images vs. the Image widget\n161\t\n162\t- **Inline markdown image** (`![alt](url)` inside a `theory.markdown` block) — for *supporting* visuals that flow with the prose: a small reference figure, a tiny diagram, an example output sample, a screenshot. Every theory section whose `markdown` is **≥ 300 characters** SHOULD include **at least one** inline image where it makes pedagogical sense (a sketch of the concept, a side-by-side comparison, a sample output). Don't force an image into a 50-word theory paragraph just to satisfy the rule — judgement first; if no image genuinely helps, skip it.\n163\t- **Image widget section** (`type: \"image\"`, US-050) — for *hero illustrations*, headline diagrams, multi-panel figures, annotated reference plots, or anything that warrants its own caption + attribution treatment. Use one when the figure IS the point of the section, not a sidebar to it. Image widget sections render as a `<figure>` with optional `<figcaption>` and an attribution line.\n164\t\n165\tA typical lesson uses **1–3 inline images** across its theory sections plus **0–2 Image widget sections** for headline figures.\n166\t\n167\t### Where to find images\n168\t\n169\tPREFER stable, redistributable sources:\n170\t\n171\t- **Wikimedia Commons** (`commons.wikimedia.org` / `upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/...`) — the default. Large, well-licensed (CC BY / CC BY-SA / public domain), stable URLs. Always link to the file's Commons description page in `attribution.url`.\n172\t- **Public-domain repositories** — NASA image gallery, USGS, NOAA, Library of Congress, NIH/NLM, government science agencies. Public domain by default.\n173\t- **Official documentation diagrams** — `scikit-image`, `OpenCV`, `matplotlib`, `numpy`, `scipy`, `pytorch`, `scikit-learn` docs and gallery pages. Every PNG/SVG the docs serve is fair game for educational reuse; credit the project.\n174\t- **arxiv figure references** — figures embedded in arxiv papers (`arxiv.org/abs/...` HTML view → figure URLs, or screenshots of paper figures with citation).\n175\t\n176\tAVOID hotlinking from rot-prone or unlicensed hosts:\n177\t\n178\t- `medium.com`, `towardsdatascience.com`, `dev.to`, personal substack/wordpress/blogspot blogs — go 404 frequently and licensing is unclear.\n179\t- Imgur, Discord CDN, Twitter/X media URLs, random Google-image-search results — not durable, not authoritative.\n180\t- Any URL that looks like a tracking redirect or shortlink.\n181\t\n182\tIf you can't find a stable image for a concept, **omit it**; do not fall back to a blog host.\n183\t\n184\t### Alt text — REQUIRED for every image\n185\t\n186\tEVERY image — inline OR widget — MUST have **meaningful alt text** describing what is shown. A good alt text answers *\"what would a screen-reader user need to know to understand this figure?\"*. Be specific about content, not just topic.\n187\t\n188\t- ✅ Good: `![Side-by-side comparison: original cameraman image (left) vs. the same image after Gaussian blur with σ=2 (right) — fine texture lost, edges softened](...)`.\n189\t- ✅ Good: `![Step edge in a 1-D signal: median filter output (orange) preserves the discontinuity exactly; mean filter output (blue) smears across two samples](...)`.\n190\t- ❌ BAD: `![image](...)`, `![figure](...)`, `![](...)` — empty or placeholder.\n191\t- ❌ BAD: `![noise](...)`, `![diagram](...)` — uninformative single-word labels.\n192\t\n193\tThe Image widget schema enforces a non-empty `alt`; inline markdown has no schema enforcement, so discipline is on you.\n194\t\n195\t### Attribution\n196\t\n197\tFor Image widget sections, populate `data.attribution` whenever the source license requires it:\n198\t\n199\t- **Wikimedia Commons** — required. Use the format `\"Wikimedia Commons, <author>, <license>\"` (e.g. `\"Wikimedia Commons, User:Cmglee, CC BY-SA 4.0\"`). Set `attribution.url` to the file's Commons description page (`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:<filename>`).\n200\t- **Official docs** (scikit-image, matplotlib, OpenCV, etc.) — recommended. Use the format `\"<Project> documentation\"` and link `attribution.url` to the page that hosts the figure.\n201\t- **NASA / USGS / NOAA / public domain** — credit by convention even when not legally required (`\"NASA / <mission>\"`, `\"USGS\"`, `\"Public domain\"`).\n202\t- **arxiv** — credit the paper (`\"<First Author> et al., arxiv:<id>\"`) and link `attribution.url` to the paper's abstract page.\n203\t\n204\tFor inline markdown images, credit briefly in the surrounding prose if license requires it (`*(Image: Wikimedia Commons, User:Foo, CC BY-SA 4.0)*`) — markdown has no dedicated attribution slot.\n205\t\n206\t### Image widget shape (recap of US-050)\n207\t\n208\t```ts\n209\t{\n210\t  type: \"image\",\n211\t  id: \"<unique>\",\n212\t  title: \"<short caption-style title>\",\n213\t  data: {\n214\t    src: \"<URL — http(s) external; cached locally on lesson save>\",\n215\t    alt: \"<meaningful description of the figure>\",        // REQUIRED, non-empty\n216\t    caption?: \"<one-sentence figure caption>\",\n217\t    attribution?: { text: \"<credit string>\", url?: \"<source page>\" },\n218\t    maxWidth?: \"<px or %, default '100%'>\"\n219\t  }\n220\t}\n221\t```\n222\t\n223\tThe Image widget caches external URLs into `courses/<slug>/assets/images/` on save (US-050), so a `commons.wikimedia.org` URL becomes a local relative path on first lesson save — lessons stay viewable offline and source-of-truth attribution is preserved.\n224\t\n225\t---\n226\t\n227\t## Step 6: Compose the Lesson\n228\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5179973-6d3e-41c1-821c-3b55ae720415","uuid":"735d45ba-9e79-4d11-b83c-c6ca32354da3","timestamp":"2026-05-04T00:56:06.290Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/nukeemann/projects/ai_lecturer/.worktrees/US-051/scripts/ralph/skills/generate_lesson/SKILL.md","content":"## Step 5: Visual Illustrations\n\nLessons should be visually rich, not walls of text. A learner who scrolls past three screen-heights of unbroken prose has already disengaged. Use **inline markdown images** inside `theory` sections for supporting visuals, and the dedicated **`image` widget** (US-050) as a standalone section for hero illustrations, diagrams, or figures that warrant their own caption + attribution treatment.\n\nPlan visuals during this step — *before* you write the section JSON — so you can weave the image references naturally into the markdown rather than bolting them on at the end.\n\n### Inline markdown images vs. the Image widget\n\n- **Inline markdown image** (`![alt](url)` inside a `theory.markdown` block) — for *supporting* visuals that flow with the prose: a small reference figure, a tiny diagram, an example output sample, a screenshot. Every theory section whose `markdown` is **≥ 300 characters** SHOULD include **at least one** inline image where it makes pedagogical sense (a sketch of the concept, a side-by-side comparison, a sample output). Don't force an image into a 50-word theory paragraph just to satisfy the rule — judgement first; if no image genuinely helps, skip it.\n- **Image widget section** (`type: \"image\"`, US-050) — for *hero illustrations*, headline diagrams, multi-panel figures, annotated reference plots, or anything that warrants its own caption + attribution treatment. Use one when the figure IS the point of the section, not a sidebar to it. Image widget sections render as a `<figure>` with optional `<figcaption>` and an attribution line.\n\nA typical lesson uses **1–3 inline images** across its theory sections plus **0–2 Image widget sections** for headline figures.\n\n### Where to find images\n\nPREFER stable, redistributable sources:\n\n- **Wikimedia Commons** (`commons.wikimedia.org` / `upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/...`) — the default. Large, well-licensed (CC BY / CC BY-SA / public domain), stable URLs. Always link to the file's Commons description page in `attribution.url`.\n- **Public-domain repositories** — NASA image gallery, USGS, NOAA, Library of Congress, NIH/NLM, government science agencies. Public domain by default.\n- **Official documentation diagrams** — `scikit-image`, `OpenCV`, `matplotlib`, `numpy`, `scipy`, `pytorch`, `scikit-learn` docs and gallery pages. Every PNG/SVG the docs serve is fair game for educational reuse; credit the project.\n- **arxiv figure references** — figures embedded in arxiv papers (`arxiv.org/abs/...` HTML view → figure URLs, or screenshots of paper figures with citation).\n\nAVOID hotlinking from rot-prone or unlicensed hosts:\n\n- `medium.com`, `towardsdatascience.com`, `dev.to`, personal substack/wordpress/blogspot blogs — go 404 frequently and licensing is unclear.\n- Imgur, Discord CDN, Twitter/X media URLs, random Google-image-search results — not durable, not authoritative.\n- Any URL that looks like a tracking redirect or shortlink.\n\nIf you can't find a stable image for a concept, **omit it**; do not fall back to a blog host.\n\n### Alt text — REQUIRED for every image\n\nEVERY image — inline OR widget — MUST have **meaningful alt text** describing what is shown. A good alt text answers *\"what would a screen-reader user need to know to understand this figure?\"*. Be specific about content, not just topic.\n\n- ✅ Good: `![Side-by-side comparison: original cameraman image (left) vs. the same image after Gaussian blur with σ=2 (right) — fine texture lost, edges softened](...)`.\n- ✅ Good: `![Step edge in a 1-D signal: median filter output (orange) preserves the discontinuity exactly; mean filter output (blue) smears across two samples](...)`.\n- ❌ BAD: `![image](...)`, `![figure](...)`, `![](...)` — empty or placeholder.\n- ❌ BAD: `![noise](...)`, `![diagram](...)` — uninformative single-word labels.\n\nThe Image widget schema enforces a non-empty `alt`; inline markdown has no schema enforcement, so discipline is on you.\n\n### Attribution\n\nFor Image widget sections, populate `data.attribution` whenever the source license requires it:\n\n- **Wikimedia Commons** — required. Use the format `\"Wikimedia Commons, <author>, <license>\"` (e.g. `\"Wikimedia Commons, User:Cmglee, CC BY-SA 4.0\"`). Set `attribution.url` to the file's Commons description page (`https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:<filename>`).\n- **Official docs** (scikit-image, matplotlib, OpenCV, etc.) — recommended. Use the format `\"<Project> documentation\"` and link `attribution.url` to the page that hosts the figure.\n- **NASA / USGS / NOAA / public domain** — credit by convention even when not legally required (`\"NASA / <mission>\"`, `\"USGS\"`, `\"Public domain\"`).\n- **arxiv** — credit the paper (`\"<First Author> et al., arxiv:<id>\"`) and link `attribution.url` to the paper's abstract page.\n\nFor inline markdown images, credit briefly in the surrounding prose if license requires it (`*(Image: Wikimedia Commons, User:Foo, CC BY-SA 4.0)*`) — markdown has no dedicated attribution slot.\n\n### Image widget shape (recap of US-050)\n\n```ts\n{\n  type: \"image\",\n  id: \"<unique>\",\n  title: \"<short caption-style title>\",\n  data: {\n    src: \"<URL — http(s) external; cached locally on lesson save>\",\n    alt: \"<meaningful description of the figure>\",        // REQUIRED, non-empty\n    caption?: \"<one-sentence figure caption>\",\n    attribution?: { text: \"<credit string>\", url?: \"<source page>\" },\n    maxWidth?: \"<px or %, default '100%'>\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe Image widget caches external URLs into `courses/<slug>/assets/images/` on save (US-050), so a `commons.wikimedia.org` URL becomes a local relative path on first lesson save — lessons stay viewable offline and source-of-truth attribution is preserved.\n\n---\n\n## Step 6: Compose the Lesson\n","numLines":75,"startLine":154,"totalLines":555}}}
